    {day:1,title:"How are traits passed from parents?",short:"Genes & Heredity",image:"assets/microscopic-world-v1.png",icon:"🧬",reward:"Heredity Code Card",rewardIcon:"🗂️",story:"Heredity is the transmission of traits from parents to offspring. The gene is a basic unit of hereditary information. Genes help control body features, growth and many functions. A trait can disappear in one generation and reappear in another depending on the genes inherited.",guide:"Mendel",guideLine:"Connect the visible trait to the information unit passed through generations.",words:[word("gene","gen","a segment of hereditary information that influences traits","noun","A gene can influence eye color."),word("heredity","di truyền","the transmission of traits from parents to offspring","noun","Heredity explains family resemblances."),word("generation","thế hệ","a group of organisms at a similar stage in a family line","noun","A trait may skip one generation.")],questions:[mcq("What is a gene?",["A unit of hereditary information","A complete body organ","A type of skin layer"],0,"Genes carry instructions that influence inherited traits.","Look for the information unit.","It is passed through heredity."),tf("Test the family-trait claim.","A trait may be absent in parents but visible in their child.",true,"Recessive inherited information can be hidden in one generation and expressed in another.","Visible appearance does not reveal every gene.","Some traits can skip a generation."),gap("Complete the inheritance note.","The transmission of traits from parent to offspring is ____.",["heredity","digestion","weathering"],0,"Heredity names the process of trait transmission.","Choose the genetics term.","It is today's second vocabulary word.")]},
    {day:2,title:"How are genes packaged inside cells?",short:"DNA & Chromosomes",image:"assets/microscopic-world-v1.png",icon:"🧫",reward:"Chromosome Package Model",rewardIcon:"🔬",story:"A gene is a segment of DNA. A long DNA molecule coils with proteins to form a chromosome inside the cell nucleus. Humans normally have 23 chromosome pairs, receiving one chromosome of each pair from each parent. DNA is copied as cells divide and the body grows.",guide:"Mendel",guideLine:"Zoom in from cell to nucleus to chromosome to DNA to gene.",words:[word("chromosome","nhiễm sắc thể","a package of DNA and protein inside a cell nucleus","noun","A human cell normally contains 23 chromosome pairs."),word("DNA","ADN","the genetic material carrying hereditary instructions","noun","Genes are segments of DNA."),word("pair","cặp","two corresponding items considered together","noun","Each chromosome pair includes one from each parent.")],questions:[matching("Match each structure to its description.",[{left:"nucleus",right:"contains chromosomes"},{left:"chromosome",right:"coiled package of DNA and protein"},{left:"gene",right:"segment of DNA"}],"Genetic information is organized at several nested levels.","Start with the cell compartment.","The smallest listed information segment is the gene."),tf("Test the parental contribution claim.","Each parent contributes one chromosome to each chromosome pair.",true,"The pair combines one maternal and one paternal chromosome.","A pair has two corresponding members.","Each parent supplies one member."),mcq("Why is DNA sometimes compared with a recipe?",["It contains instructions used to build and maintain an organism","It is eaten by cells as food","It is stored only in kitchens"],0,"DNA encodes biological instructions.","Think about what a recipe provides.","Both supply directions for making something.")]},
    {day:3,title:"How can one gene hide another?",short:"Dominant & Recessive",image:"assets/microscopic-world-v1.png",icon:"🔀",reward:"Gene Pair Simulator",rewardIcon:"⚙️",story:"Genes occur in pairs because one member comes from each parent. A dominant form can be expressed when one or two copies are present. A recessive form is usually visible only when both copies in the pair are recessive. The gene pair, not a single family resemblance, determines which form is expressed.",guide:"Mendel",guideLine:"Check both members of the pair before predicting the visible trait.",words:[word("dominant","trội","expressed when at least one copy is present","adjective","A dominant trait can appear with one dominant gene form."),word("recessive","lặn","usually expressed only when both copies are recessive","adjective","Blue eyes are used as a recessive example in this lesson."),word("expressed","được biểu hiện","made visible or active as a trait","adjective","The dominant form is expressed in the offspring.")],questions:[mcq("When is a recessive trait usually visible?",["When both gene copies are recessive","Whenever one dominant copy is present","Only when no genes exist"],0,"A dominant copy usually masks the recessive form.","Check both members of the pair.","Two recessive copies are needed."),matching("Match each pair to the expected expression in a simple dominant-recessive model.",[{left:"two dominant copies",right:"dominant trait"},{left:"one dominant and one recessive",right:"dominant trait"},{left:"two recessive copies",right:"recessive trait"}],"One dominant copy is enough to express the dominant form.","Start with the two matching pairs.","The mixed pair still shows the dominant trait."),tf("Test the hidden-gene claim.","A person showing a dominant trait can still carry a recessive gene form.",true,"A mixed gene pair expresses the dominant form while retaining the recessive copy.","Visible traits do not show the whole pair.","One copy can be hidden." )]},
    {day:4,title:"Do genes alone decide handedness?",short:"Genes & Environment",image:"assets/science-germs-immune-defense-v1.png",icon:"✋",reward:"Handedness Evidence Board",rewardIcon:"📊",story:"Genes influence whether a person tends to use the left or right hand, but environment and experience can also affect behavior. Tools designed for right-handed users, cultural customs and practice may change which hand a person uses for some tasks. Traits can reflect both inherited information and environmental influence.",guide:"Mendel",guideLine:"Avoid a one-cause answer: weigh genetic tendency with experience and culture.",words:[word("environment","môi trường","surrounding conditions and experiences that affect an organism","noun","The environment can influence which hand is practiced."),word("influence","ảnh hưởng","to affect an outcome without necessarily determining it completely","verb","Culture can influence daily hand use."),word("custom","phong tục","a traditional way of behaving in a group","noun","A social custom may favor the right hand.")],questions:[mcq("Which is an environmental influence on handedness?",["Using tools designed for right-handed people","Receiving chromosomes from parents","A gene located on DNA"],0,"Tool design is an external condition that can change practiced behavior.","Look outside the body.","A manufactured tool is part of the environment."),tf("Test the one-cause claim.","Genes are the only possible influence on which hand a person uses.",false,"Practice, tools and culture can also affect hand use.","Recall the examples beyond inheritance.","Environment contributes too."),gap("Complete the conclusion.","Genes may affect handedness, but experience can also ____ it.",["influence","digest","condense"],0,"Influence means affect an outcome.","Choose the word for changing a tendency.","It does not mean total control.")]},
    {day:5,title:"Why are some people left-handed?",short:"Handedness Review",image:"assets/microscopic-world-v1.png",icon:"🔬",reward:"Grade 6 Heredity Core",rewardIcon:"💎",story:"Genes are DNA segments packaged in chromosome pairs. One chromosome in each pair comes from each parent. Dominant and recessive forms help explain how some traits appear, but handedness also responds to tools, customs and practice. Both heredity and environment can shape the final behavior.",guide:"Mendel",guideLine:"Connect the genetic pathway, then add environmental influence without claiming either one acts alone.",words:[word("genetic","thuộc di truyền","relating to genes or heredity","adjective","Hand preference has a genetic component."),word("combination","sự kết hợp","two or more things joined together","noun","A gene pair is a combination from both parents."),word("probability","xác suất","the chance that an outcome will occur","noun","A gene model predicts probability, not certainty for every complex trait.")],questions:[matching("Match each term to its level.",[{left:"gene",right:"segment of DNA"},{left:"chromosome",right:"package of DNA and protein"},{left:"heredity",right:"transmission of traits"}],"The terms describe information, its package and its transmission.","Start with the smallest segment.","The process across generations is heredity."),tf("Test the chromosome claim.","A chromosome pair normally contains contributions from both parents.",true,"One chromosome of a pair comes from each parent.","A pair combines two sources.","Each parent contributes one."),gap("Complete the gene-pair rule.","A ____ trait is usually visible only when both copies are recessive.",["recessive","dominant","fertile"],0,"A dominant copy masks a recessive form in this simple model.","Two matching copies are required.","Choose the hidden form."),mcq("Which answer best explains handedness?",["Inherited genes contribute, while environment and practice can also influence hand use","Only scissors determine it","One chromosome always decides it with no other influence"],0,"The book presents handedness as a trait influenced by both heredity and environment.","Avoid an absolute one-cause answer.","Use nature and experience together."),mcq("If one dominant and one recessive copy occur in a simple gene pair, which form is expressed?",["Dominant","Recessive only","Neither"],0,"One dominant copy is enough to express the dominant form.","Recall Day 3.","The recessive copy can remain hidden.")]}
