  const week24=[
    null,
    {day:1,title:"Why can kernels on corn differ?",short:"Corn Traits",image:"assets/garden-room-v1.png",icon:"🌽",reward:"Kernel Trait Tray",rewardIcon:"🗂️",story:"Each corn kernel is a seed with its own combination of genes. Those genes influence traits such as kernel color, sweetness, ear size, plant height and growth rate. Because kernels can inherit different combinations, plants grown from one ear may not be identical.",guide:"Barbara",guideLine:"Treat each kernel as one seed carrying its own inherited information.",words:[word("kernel","hạt ngô","an individual corn seed","noun","One corn kernel can grow into a plant."),word("variety","giống","a form of a species with a distinctive set of traits","noun","Blue corn is one cultivated variety."),word("variation","sự biến đổi","a difference in a trait among individuals","noun","Kernel color shows variation within corn.")],questions:[mcq("What is one corn kernel?",["A seed with its own genes","A complete field of plants","A type of root"],0,"Each kernel can germinate into a new corn plant.","Think about what is planted.","One kernel is one seed."),tf("Test the identical-plant claim.","All kernels from one ear must produce plants with exactly the same traits.",false,"Individual seeds may contain different gene combinations.","Each kernel has its own inherited set.","Variation can occur within one ear."),matching("Match each example to a corn trait.",[{left:"yellow or blue",right:"kernel color"},{left:"short or tall",right:"plant height"},{left:"fast or slow",right:"growth rate"}],"Corn varies in several visible and measurable traits.","Match color first.","Growth rate describes speed over time.")]},
    {day:2,title:"What causes genetic variation?",short:"Genes Change & Shuffle",image:"assets/garden-room-v1.png",icon:"🧬",reward:"Variation Cause Map",rewardIcon:"🗺️",story:"Genetic variation means inherited differences among members of a species. Genes can mutate into new forms. During reproduction, existing genes can be shuffled into new combinations. New genes can also enter a plant population when pollen moves between locations.",guide:"Barbara",guideLine:"Separate three causes: mutation, recombination and movement of genes.",words:[word("genetic variation","biến dị di truyền","differences in inherited traits among members of a species","noun phrase","Kernel colors show genetic variation."),word("mutate","đột biến","to undergo a lasting change in genetic material","verb","A gene can mutate into a new form."),word("pollen","phấn hoa","plant material that carries male reproductive cells","noun","Wind can carry pollen into another cornfield.")],questions:[matching("Match each cause of variation.",[{left:"mutation",right:"creates a changed gene"},{left:"gene shuffling",right:"forms a new combination"},{left:"pollen movement",right:"introduces genes from another population"}],"Several genetic processes increase variation.","Start with a direct change in DNA.","Pollen can move between fields."),mcq("Which event introduces genes from another corn population?",["Pollen arrives from a different field","A leaf loses water","A root absorbs minerals"],0,"Pollen can carry genetic material between plant populations.","Look for reproduction between locations.","The moving material is pollen."),tf("Test the causation claim.","Genetic variation can result when genes are shuffled into new combinations.",true,"Recombination can create trait combinations not seen in either parent.","The genes need not be newly created.","Existing forms can be rearranged.")]},
    {day:3,title:"How can jumping genes change kernel color?",short:"Jumping Genes",image:"assets/garden-room-v1.png",icon:"🔀",reward:"Transposon Color Model",rewardIcon:"⚙️",story:"Some DNA sequences called transposons can move to new positions. A transposon may switch off a nearby color gene. If it moves again, the color gene may become active. This on-and-off pattern can make a kernel mottled with blue and white areas.",guide:"Barbara",guideLine:"Track where the moving DNA lands and whether the nearby color gene is active.",words:[word("transposon","yếu tố chuyển vị","a DNA sequence that can move to a new position","noun","A transposon can affect a nearby gene."),word("mottled","lốm đốm","marked with spots or patches of different colors","adjective","The kernel has a mottled blue-and-white pattern."),word("active gene","gen hoạt động","a gene currently being used to produce its effect","noun phrase","An active color gene makes blue pigment.")],questions:[mcq("What can happen when a transposon lands beside a color gene?",["The color gene may be switched off","The kernel becomes an animal","All DNA leaves the cell"],0,"The moving sequence can block the nearby gene's activity.","Look at the effect on gene expression.","The gene remains present but may be off."),gap("Complete the color description.","A kernel with blue and white patches is ____.",["mottled","sterile","fertile"],0,"Mottled means spotted or patched with different colors.","Choose the pattern word.","It describes mixed patches."),tf("Test the location claim.","Moving a transposon can change whether a nearby gene is active.",true,"Gene activity depends partly on the transposon's position.","The DNA sequence can move.","A new location can release or block the gene.")]},
    {day:4,title:"How do farmers strengthen a desired trait?",short:"Selective Breeding",image:"assets/garden-room-v1.png",icon:"🌱",reward:"Breeding Selection Chart",rewardIcon:"📊",story:"Selective breeding is human-directed mating for desirable traits. Farmers choose parent plants that strongly show a useful feature. From their offspring, they repeatedly select and breed the plants that best display that trait while preventing others from reproducing. Over generations, the desired trait becomes more common.",guide:"Barbara",guideLine:"Selection must be repeated across generations; one choice is only the first step.",words:[word("selective breeding","chọn giống","human-directed reproduction for desired traits","noun phrase","Selective breeding produced many corn varieties."),word("desirable","đáng mong muốn","wanted because it is useful or beneficial","adjective","Drought resistance is a desirable crop trait."),word("cultivate","canh tác","to grow and care for plants deliberately","verb","Farmers cultivate corn for food.")],questions:[mcq("What is the first step in selective breeding?",["Choose parents showing the desired trait","Remove all variation from the species","Change weather conditions"],0,"Breeders begin with organisms that already display useful variation.","Selection starts before mating.","Choose the parents carefully."),tf("Test the repetition claim.","Selective breeding usually requires choices repeated over several generations.",true,"Repeated selection increases the frequency and strength of the desired trait.","One generation may not fix the trait.","The process continues with offspring."),gap("Complete the crop goal.","A useful feature that farmers want is a ____ trait.",["desirable","sterile","temporary"],0,"Desirable means wanted or beneficial.","Choose the positive selection target.","It may involve color, yield or resistance.")]},
    {day:5,title:"How can corn be yellow, white or blue?",short:"Corn Genetics Review",image:"assets/garden-room-v1.png",icon:"🔬",reward:"Grade 6 Corn Core",rewardIcon:"💎",story:"Corn contains extensive genetic variation. Mutation, gene shuffling and pollen movement create different gene combinations. Moving transposons can switch color genes on or off, creating mottled kernels. For thousands of years, farmers have used selective breeding to strengthen useful colors and other traits.",guide:"Barbara",guideLine:"Combine natural genetic processes with repeated human selection.",words:[word("recombine","tái tổ hợp","to arrange genetic material into a new combination","verb","Genes recombine during reproduction."),word("trait frequency","tần suất đặc điểm","how common a trait is in a population","noun phrase","Selection can increase a trait's frequency."),word("population","quần thể","members of one species living in the same area","noun","Pollen can carry genes into a corn population.")],questions:[matching("Match each process to its result.",[{left:"mutation",right:"new genetic change"},{left:"transposon movement",right:"nearby gene switched on or off"},{left:"selective breeding",right:"desired trait becomes more common"}],"Natural changes and human choices both affect corn traits.","Start with the new DNA change.","Farmers control selection, not mutation."),tf("Test the seed claim.","Each corn kernel can carry a different combination of genes.",true,"A kernel is an individual seed produced through reproduction.","One ear contains many seeds.","Gene combinations can vary."),gap("Complete the farmer's method.","Repeatedly choosing parents with useful traits is ____ breeding.",["selective","recessive","monozygotic"],0,"Selective breeding is directed by human choice.","Choose the method from Day 4.","It strengthens desired traits."),mcq("Why can one kernel be both blue and white?",["A transposon can switch a blue-color gene off in some cells","The kernel belongs to two species","The blue pigment is always painted on"],0,"Changes in the timing and location of gene activity create mottling.","Think about the jumping gene.","Some areas have the color gene active and others do not."),mcq("Which explanation best answers the weekly question?",["Genetic variation, transposons and selective breeding produce different kernel colors","Corn has no genes controlling color","Water alone permanently chooses every kernel color"],0,"The full explanation combines inherited variation, gene activity and human selection.","Use evidence from all four days.","More than one process contributes.")]}
  ];

  const week25=[
    null,
    {day:1,title:"How do identical twins begin?",short:"Twin Formation",image:"assets/microscopic-world-v1.png",icon:"👯",reward:"Twin Formation Diagram",rewardIcon:"🗺️",story:"Identical twins are monozygotic: they begin when one fertilized egg splits into two embryos. Fraternal twins begin with two separate fertilized eggs. Identical twins are always the same sex, while fraternal twins may be the same or different sexes.",guide:"Ada",guideLine:"Count the fertilized eggs first, then count the embryos.",words:[word("embryo","phôi","an unborn organism in an early stage of development","noun","Each embryo continues to grow after the split."),word("monozygotic","cùng trứng","formed from one fertilized egg","adjective","Identical twins are monozygotic."),word("fraternal twins","sinh đôi khác trứng","twins formed from two separately fertilized eggs","plural noun","Fraternal twins are genetically similar to other siblings.")],questions:[matching("Match each twin type to its beginning.",[{left:"identical twins",right:"one fertilized egg splits"},{left:"fraternal twins",right:"two separate eggs are fertilized"},{left:"embryo",right:"early developing offspring"}],"The number of fertilized eggs distinguishes the twin types.","Mono means one.","Fraternal twins begin separately."),mcq("Why are identical twins called monozygotic?",["They form from one fertilized egg","They always form from two eggs","They have no chromosomes"],0,"Mono means one, and the original zygote divides.","Use the prefix mono-.","Count the starting eggs."),tf("Test the sex claim.","Identical twins can be one boy and one girl.",false,"Because they begin with the same fertilized egg and chromosomes, they are the same sex.","Compare their shared genetic starting point.","Fraternal twins may differ in sex; identical twins do not.")]},
    {day:2,title:"Why do identical twins share so much DNA?",short:"Shared Genome",image:"assets/microscopic-world-v1.png",icon:"🧬",reward:"Genome Copy Record",rewardIcon:"🗂️",story:"The original fertilized egg contains chromosome pairs inherited from both parents. When it divides into two embryos, both receive copies of the same DNA. Therefore identical twins begin with the same genome. Fraternal twins start from separate eggs and share about as much genetic material as other siblings.",guide:"Ada",guideLine:"Trace both twins back to the same original genome.",words:[word("genome","bộ gen","the complete set of genetic material in an organism","noun","Identical twins begin with the same genome."),word("genetic material","vật chất di truyền","DNA carrying hereditary information","noun phrase","Siblings share some genetic material."),word("copy","bản sao","a reproduction of original information","noun","Each new cell receives a DNA copy.")],questions:[mcq("Why do identical twins begin with the same genome?",["Both embryos come from the same fertilized egg","They receive DNA from different species","Their environments create matching chromosomes"],0,"The single zygote's DNA is copied into both embryos.","Return to the shared starting cell.","One egg provides one original genome."),tf("Test the sibling comparison.","Fraternal twins share about as much genetic material as siblings born at different times.",true,"They arise from two separate eggs and separate sperm, like other siblings.","They are twins because of timing, not one zygote.","Their genetic relationship is sibling-level."),gap("Complete the genetics note.","The complete set of genes in an organism is its ____.",["genome","epidermis","habitat"],0,"Genome names the entire genetic set.","Choose the whole collection, not one gene.","It is today's first vocabulary word.")]},
    {day:3,title:"How can environment make twins different?",short:"Life Experiences",image:"assets/science-germs-immune-defense-v1.png",icon:"🌍",reward:"Nature-Nurture Evidence",rewardIcon:"📊",story:"Even before birth, twins may receive different amounts of oxygen and nutrients through a shared placenta, affecting size. After birth, different friends, activities, diets and experiences can influence interests, personality and appearance. The same starting DNA does not create identical life histories.",guide:"Ada",guideLine:"Compare environmental influences before birth and after birth.",words:[word("placenta","nhau thai","an organ that supplies a developing fetus with oxygen and nutrients","noun","Twins may receive different amounts through one placenta."),word("life experience","trải nghiệm sống","an event or condition that influences development","noun phrase","Different life experiences can shape interests."),word("environmental influence","ảnh hưởng môi trường","an outside condition affecting a trait or behavior","noun phrase","Nutrition is an environmental influence on growth.")],questions:[matching("Match each influence to its stage.",[{left:"unequal nutrients from placenta",right:"before birth"},{left:"different peer groups",right:"after birth"},{left:"eye color genes",right:"inherited starting information"}],"Twin differences can begin both before and after birth.","The placenta acts during pregnancy.","Peers influence life after birth."),mcq("Why might one identical twin weigh more at birth?",["It may have received more nutrients through the placenta","It started as a different species","It had no genome"],0,"The prenatal environment can differ even inside the same womb.","Think about nutrient supply.","Shared space does not guarantee equal delivery."),tf("Test the experience claim.","Identical DNA guarantees identical interests and personalities.",false,"Experiences and environments can influence development and behavior.","Genes are not the only influence.","Twins can meet different people and make different choices.")]},
    {day:4,title:"How can gene activity change as twins age?",short:"Epigenome",image:"assets/microscopic-world-v1.png",icon:"🎚️",reward:"Epigenome Activity Map",rewardIcon:"🗺️",story:"Chemical marks can raise or lower the activity of genes without replacing the DNA sequence. The record of these changes is the epigenome. Identical twins begin with similar epigenomes, but their patterns can become less alike as they age. Copy errors during early development can also create small DNA differences.",guide:"Ada",guideLine:"Distinguish changing gene activity from changing the DNA letters themselves.",words:[word("epigenome","hệ biểu sinh","the pattern of chemical marks that affect gene activity","noun","The twins' epigenomes become less alike over time."),word("gene activity","hoạt động gen","the degree to which a gene is being used","noun phrase","A chemical mark can lower gene activity."),word("copy error","lỗi sao chép","a change introduced while DNA is copied","noun phrase","A copy error can create a small genetic difference.")],questions:[mcq("What does the epigenome mainly affect?",["How active certain genes are","Whether the body contains any DNA","The number of planets in space"],0,"Chemical marks act like controls that increase or decrease gene use.","Think of a gas pedal or brake.","The genes remain but their activity changes."),tf("Test the age claim.","Identical twins' epigenomes can become less similar as they age.",true,"Different experiences and chemical changes accumulate over time.","Compare birth with later life.","The patterns do not stay perfectly matched."),gap("Complete the mutation note.","A small DNA difference can begin as a copying ____.",["error","organ","species"],0,"An error made while DNA is copied can become a mutation.","Choose the word describing an inaccurate copy.","It is today's third vocabulary phrase.")]},
    {day:5,title:"Are identical twins exactly alike?",short:"Twin Evidence Review",image:"assets/microscopic-world-v1.png",icon:"🔬",reward:"Grade 6 Twin Core",rewardIcon:"💎",story:"Identical twins begin from one fertilized egg and share nearly all their starting DNA. Yet prenatal conditions, life experiences, epigenetic changes and occasional copy errors can create differences. They are much more genetically alike than fraternal twins, but they are not exact copies in every trait or experience.",guide:"Ada",guideLine:"Answer with both similarity and difference: shared origin does not erase later change.",words:[word("nature","bẩm sinh","genetic influences on development","noun","Nature gives identical twins a shared genetic starting point."),word("nurture","nuôi dưỡng","environmental and experiential influences on development","noun","Nurture can shape different interests."),word("identical","giống hệt","exactly the same in every detail","adjective","The word identical can be misleading for twins.")],questions:[matching("Match each source of similarity or difference.",[{left:"same fertilized egg",right:"shared starting genome"},{left:"different experiences",right:"environmental differences"},{left:"epigenome changes",right:"different gene activity"}],"Twin development reflects both shared heredity and changing conditions.","Start with their common origin.","Chemical marks affect activity."),tf("Test the exact-copy claim.","Identical twins are exact copies in every trait and experience.",false,"Environment, epigenetic changes and copy errors can create differences.","The term identical is partly misleading.","Shared DNA is not a shared life."),gap("Complete the origin note.","Identical twins are ____ because they form from one fertilized egg.",["monozygotic","sterile","selectively bred"],0,"Monozygotic means formed from a single zygote.","Use the word from Day 1.","Mono means one."),mcq("Which factor can make identical twins different before birth?",["Unequal nutrients through the placenta","Belonging to different species","Having no chromosomes"],0,"Even a shared womb can provide unequal conditions.","Focus on the prenatal environment.","The placenta supplies nutrients."),mcq("Which answer best explains the weekly question?",["They share a genome at first, but environment, epigenome and copy errors create differences","They are unrelated organisms","They share no DNA but have identical experiences"],0,"The complete explanation includes both common genetic origin and later divergence.","Use nature and nurture.","Similarity and difference are both supported by evidence.")]}
  ];

